{0 idk}

IDKit, contact data for OCaml. One package per specification, and the JSContact
Card of {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9553.html}RFC 9553} as the
representation they meet at.

{!Idk} is the entry point. It explains how the layers stack, and every package
below is reached through it. {!Idk_eio} holds the CardDAV and CalDAV clients.

- [jscontact] is the Card and every object type of
  {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9553.html} RFC 9553}, with the conversion
  to and from vCard of {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9555.html} RFC 9555}
  in [jscontact.vcard].
- [vcard] is vCard 4.0,
  {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6350.html} RFC 6350}, with
  {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6868.html} RFC 6868} and
  {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9554.html} RFC 9554}.
- [ical] is iCalendar,
  {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5545.html} RFC 5545}, with
  {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7986.html} RFC 7986}.
- [carddav] is CardDAV,
  {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6352.html} RFC 6352}, on [httpz.dav] and
  [fetch.dav], with a client on Eio and a view of address objects as JSContact
  cards.
- [caldav] is CalDAV, {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4791.html} RFC 4791},
  with a client on Eio.

A vCard read from a file, an address object fetched over CardDAV and a
[ContactCard] received over JMAP all become the same {!Idk.Jscontact.Card.t},
and a Card written back to any of them loses nothing it arrived with.

{1 Mirroring a collection}

The [idk-dav-mirror] executable copies an address book or a calendar to a
directory and keeps it up to date with the sync-collection report of
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6578.html}RFC 6578}. Its source is the
shortest complete example of the clients.
